# Taking a lot of time off work
If you've been keeping up, in my recent blog posts I've been mentioning that I recently drastically cut my work hours down. I now only work 10-14 hours/week, spread out over 2-3 days. The cut is so drastic that even with my supportive partner paying a higher % into rent then me, I'll still be in the red month after month. I'm planning to keep to bleed money for the next 2-6 months.

## How did I get here?
I won't go into the deep details, but I've had a bad 3 or 4 years mental health wise. One day when I was visiting a mall I had a sudden and intense panic attack, which I had never experienced before. After that day, I suddenly developed an intense [agoraphobia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agoraphobia).

At most times of the day I was managing some sort of strong anxiety or panic; I had to work very hard to distract myself from it. I found that going outside my apartment made things much worse, especially if I was in a noisy/crowded space. I also couldn't go to sleep unless I was completely physically exhausted, I had some sort of panic instinct that would prevent me from willingly giving up consciousness for any reason. I ended up with an ever shifting sleep schedule that involved staying up 18+ hours often as a result. For the first couple of months it was extremely difficult to deal with. As time went on, I slowly got better at managing it.

Thankfully, I worked a remote job at the time. I did software development for a small startup company and for the most part I didn't have any required times I had to be online. Because I got to work when I wanted, I got to exploit the bits of time during my day when I could focus in order to get my daily work done. I'm forever grateful I got to have that job during the  time it was the worst, I don't think any other job could have accommodated my struggles quite so seamlessly. They paid me fairly well given my low amount of previous professional experience too!

The startup company I worked for was pre-revenue and was running low on funds. The founder started looking everywhere they could for additional investment and I could see the writing on the wall. In order to make sure the company could continue operating, the founder was forced to make the decision to lay off all of its workers (including me).

I had no idea what had triggered my original panic attack that caused such intense regression. In the back of my mind I had a worry that one day another huge panic attack would hit me and reverse all of my progress I had made. Because of that concern, I was focused on saving money. The idea was, if I had another regression that made me unable to work, I would have the financial ability to quit and ride on saving for some time.

As soon as I learned my contract with the startup company would be terminated I started looking for the first gig I could get as a temporary in-between job. I wanted to protect my savings as fiercely as possible and minimize any gap in employment. I got a job at a retail shipping store just in time to have zero gap in employment. Shipping is sort of one of my "things" so to say, as you may be able to tell from my USPS vehicle model collection:

(insert image of collection here)

Or my stamp collection:

(insert image of stamp collection here)

What wasn't my thing however, was being in retail stores... Or any public space with other people to be honest. I decided I'd just dive in head first and hope exposure therapy would work it's magic. I've been able to get over certain aspects of my anxiety through relentless exposure therapy before, so I figured it might be good for me (even if it's painful).

My first few days working there I had a really hard time focusing, there was just too much going on at once for my mind to process it all. Those first few nights after work I had the most vivid dreams I had ever had in my life. They were so realistic I had a hard time distinguishing them from reality, to the point that I was sometimes waking up my partner from talking in my sleep. It was pretty difficult to deal with, but pushed through knowing I have savings to fall back on if it doesn't work out. Plus, I only really planned to work there temporarily for around 6-12 months anyway!

(insert 30 months later graphic here)

Well, it's been 2 and a half years, and I'm still here. The exposure therapy has worked some magic! I don't panic much anymore, I am much better hooked into queues of when I need to step away from a situation, and I can handle much more than I could before starting the job. I consider myself quite good at my job too! Unexpectedly, it's turned out to be quite knowledge dependent and demanding of your ability to self-teach. I love any job where I get to feel like I'm advancing some sort of repository of personal knowledge! (even if, some of it was due to the owner not providing proper training...). I also had to build skills working with people who where angry/had unreasonable demands (both dealing with customers, and the owner :p). It turns out shipping brings out the worst in people at times haha. Anyway, dealing with all of this is a huge deal-breaker for most people. On average, the store has a complete team turnover every 6 months or so. It's pretty rare for anyone to stick around here for over a year.

The whole time I've worked here it's been quite painful. A lot of the pain has been a necessary evil in order to advance my progress with anxiety, but over the past year or so much of the pain hasn't felt like it's come with any reward of progress. Not only that, but things are actually getting worse the more I work. Stress from work has started causing completely disabling stomach aches which keep me up late at night. I have a fairly high tolerance for dealing with mental anguish, but having it also punch me in the gut at the same time feels just unhealthy and unacceptable.

I gave it some time, and when it became clear that there was truly no compromise or workaround possible and that the stomach pain was 100% due to stress/anxiety I decided I had to leave. I didn't really want to flat out quit with no backup, so instead I decided to just cut my hours down a lot so I'd still have some amount of income.

## So, what now?
Now that I have a pocket of valuable inbetween time, I'm doing my best to utilize it as best as possible. Right now, I'm focusing on improving my day to day mental health and personal habits.

A big side effect of my anxiety is a complete inability to focus. Back when my anxiety was much worse, I coped with it my distracting myself. That got me hooked on distracting myself. Now, any amount of anxiety gives me a subliminal urge to do something distracting. As a result, I often end up spending free days distracting myself and doing mostly nothing all day. I pretty much have a mental dependence on distracting myself.

My first order of business has been untraining my distraction reflex. I've been allocating more time to doing projects, I've started going to the library on every single non-work day, and I've made a goose themed sticker calendar to track my progress. If I went "above and beyond" on a day I earn a goose sticker to put on the calendar that day.

I'm under no illusion that I'll be able to totally fix everything, but I do think that I'll at least be able to significantly reclaim my ability to focus if I put enough into it.